    'b2.closewdv': "Closing WDV", 'b2.gain': "gain {a}: ask your accountant", 'b2.farules': "How depreciation is worked out", 'b2.farule1': "Book depreciation follows the Companies Act, 2013, Schedule II: each kind of asset has a useful life (for example computers 3 years, furniture 10 years, plant and machinery 15 years, cars 8 years), the residual value is usually not more than 5% of cost, and an asset added or sold during the year gets depreciation only for the days it was used. Choose straight line (SLM: the same amount every year) or written down value (WDV: a fixed rate on what is left). Proprietors and firms often use the same method; your accountant decides.",
    'b2.farule2': "Income-tax depreciation is worked out separately for each block of assets: written down value at the block rate (for example 15% plant and machinery, 10% furniture, 40% computers), half the rate for an asset put to use for less than 180 days in the year, and sale money taken off the block. It is for your income-tax return only and is never posted in the books. The rates filled in are the usual block rates: confirm them with your accountant, especially for years from 1 April 2026 under the Income-tax Act, 2025.",
    'b2.farule3': "Depreciation reaches your accounts only when you tap Post depreciation: a journal entry dated the last day of the year (or today, during the year) debits Depreciation and lowers each asset. If you change an asset later, post again and only the difference is added.",
